<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The texture graph's evaluator: a plan of compute kernels over a pool of intermediate
            images, dispatched on a graphics device and read back as pixels. No graph, no document,
            no panel — the artefact both front ends compile to, and the thing that runs it.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;texture-graph;material-authoring;compute;raven;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
          **The compile-time reference set is exactly what is named below, and nothing else.** That is
          why `Vixen.Core` and `Vixen.Core.Mathematics` appear there — they used to arrive through
          `Vixen.Graphics` — and why `Vixen.Core.Syntax` does, which used to arrive through Raven. It
          does *not* keep the interface framework out of the output directory, which the note on the
          node-graph reference is about: what it keeps out is this assembly's ability to *spell*
          `UiElement`, and doc 48 § 4.1's refusal of an `Svg Path` node rests on that.

          ⚠ **It also used to be what made `TexturePlanEvaluator.cs` compile, and that is no longer
          true** — [#717](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/717). That file wrote
          `Core.Imaging.Bitmap`, a *relative* name resolved by walking out from
          `namespace Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph` until something called `Core` turns up: hiding
          `Vixen.Editor.Core` from the compilation was the only reason the walk still found
          `Vixen.Core`, so the file compiled by accident and turning this property off failed a file
          nobody had edited. The names are spelled `Vixen.Core.Imaging.…` now, and this property
          answers for the compile surface alone. ⚠ **The issue's own prescription — that the property
          and those two references come out with the qualification — is refused here**: since it was
          filed, doc 48 § 4.1 made the property load-bearing for a decision that has nothing to do
          with a namespace collision.
        -->
        <DisableTransitiveProjectReferences>true</DisableTransitiveProjectReferences>

        <!--
          CA1051 (no visible instance fields) is the one rule a node library cannot keep, for the
          reason `Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph` states: a node's ports *are* its fields, which is what
          lets the generator read the declaration once. The nodes here are internal, so the rule does
          not fire today; the line is here so that widening one for a panel is not also a build
          failure nobody predicted.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1051</NoWarn>

        <!--
          ⚠ **Back on, against the TOOLING profile's default, and #821 is what that default cost.**
          `Directory.Build.props` turns documentation generation off for `Editor/**`, `Tools/**` and
          `Raven/**` — so nothing in those trees emits CS1570, CS1573, CS1574 or CS1734, and every
          `<see cref>` in ~43 node classes, 46 kernel files and the compiler was unchecked. Ask what
          that gate prints on the day it does not run: `0 Warning(s)`. Switching it on here found
          three broken crefs and a malformed `<remarks>` immediately, and this assembly is the one
          doc 48 is building, so it is where the instrument goes on first.

          CS1591 and CS1573 are the "undocumented public member" and "undocumented parameter" rules,
          which are a question about a *packaged* API and not about whether a link resolves; they
          are suppressed so that the rules that catch a lie stay on and the rule that asks for prose
          stays off.
        -->
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The kernels are embedded rather than compiled to a committed `.spv` beside the source,
          and that is a deliberate departure from `Editor/Vixen.Editor.Host/Shaders`. A storage
          image's texel format is part of its *type* in both SPIR-V and GLSL, and Raven's
          `[Permutation]` values are bool, int and uint — so one kernel that writes `rgba8` in one
          plan and `rgba16f` in the next cannot be one module. The variants are produced from this
          text at load, which means there is no committed binary anybody can leave stale, and
          `CheckShaders`' inventory asks for an entry only where one exists.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Shaders\*.rvn" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **And the shader library's own sources, verbatim, so that a kernel can `import` instead
          of transcribing** — [#635](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/635). The issue offers a
          compiled `.rvnlib` or a prelude this assembly writes; both are answers to a cause that is
          not the cause. `RavenEffectCompiler.FromSources` takes a *set* of texts and makes them one
          compilation, and a package's declarations are visible across one compilation — the
          evaluator was passing one text. So these are the library's files at their own path rather
          than a copy of them, which is what makes the count of copies zero and not two.

          ⚠ `LogicalName` is load-bearing twice. `TextureKernels` reads every embedded resource under
          `Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Shaders.` ending in `.rvn` and *that set is the kernel list*, so
          a prelude source landing under that prefix would register as a kernel with no
          `[ComputeShader]` and no `[Format]`. And a linked file's default resource name is derived
          from its path relative to this project, which for `..\..\Raven\…` is not a name anybody
          would predict.

          `Core/Math.rvn` is here because `Core/Random.rvn` spells `Math.SphericalToCartesian` and
          `Const.TwoPi` — a set that stops at `Random.rvn` fails RVN2010 on *every* kernel, because a
          library file in the compilation is bound whether the kernel calls that part of it or not.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Core\Math.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Prelude.Core.Math.rvn" />
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Core\Random.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Prelude.Core.Random.rvn" />
        <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\Raven\Library\Material\ComputeColor.rvn"
                          LogicalName="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Prelude.Material.ComputeColor.rvn" />

        <!--
          And doc 48 § D5's other half: the compounds. § 4.9's catalogue is content rather than code,
          so a `Histogram Scan` is a `.vxtexgraph` beside the kernels rather than a node class — and
          it is embedded for the same reason they are, which is that there is then nothing a
          deployment can leave behind and no folder anybody has to find. `TextureCompoundLibrary`
          reads them; its `Root` is this folder's name, so a file that moves out of it stops shipping
          rather than shipping under a wrong path.

          ⚠ A shipped compound's file name may not contain a dot. A manifest resource name has no way
          to tell a folder separator from one, so `Grunge v2.vxtexgraph` would publish under
          `Grunge v2` with a phantom folder in it. `TextureCompoundLibraryTests` holds that.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Compounds\**\*.vxtexgraph" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          A device to allocate images on and dispatch over, the effect model that says what a
          compiled variant binds, and the in-process Raven compiler the editor already resolves its
          own variants through — the same three `Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph` takes, for the same
          reason: a device is not a UI.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics\Vixen.Graphics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Shaders\Vixen.Shaders.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Tools\Vixen.ShaderCompiler\Vixen.ShaderCompiler.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ The Raven *front end*, on top of the effect compiler above, and the two answer different
          questions. `RavenEffectCompiler` compiles a whole shader to bytecode and throws a string on
          failure; doc 48 § D6 needs Raven's own `Diagnostic` objects — with a `Location` a line
          number comes out of — so that a complaint about generated text names the node whose
          expression is on that line, and it needs `FieldSymbol.ConstantValue` so that a scalar
          parameter's expression is folded by the real compiler rather than by a second evaluator
          this repository would then own. `Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors` takes the same reference for the
          same first reason. It is already here at runtime, transitively through the effect compiler;
          this only makes it visible to the compilation.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Raven\Vixen.Raven\Vixen.Raven.csproj" />

        <!-- And the syntax layer a `Diagnostic` and its `Location` are declared in, which
             `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above stops arriving through Raven itself. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Syntax\Vixen.Core.Syntax.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the image containers, because a bake's last step is a file. `PngCodec` sits under
          everything that writes a picture in this repository precisely so that a new writer does
          not become a new encoder.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Imaging\Vixen.Core.Imaging.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **And the YAML reader, which this assembly deliberately did not have.**
          `NodeGraphDocument`'s own remarks say the node-graph framework "does not choose a file
          format" — its asset records are `[DataContract]`s a caller may write as YAML or bake with
          the binary serializer — and that argument holds for a *document*, which some host owns.
          It does not hold for the compounds of § 4.9: those are files this assembly **ships**, so
          this assembly is the caller, and something here has to read them.

          Measured rather than assumed: `Vixen.Core.Yaml.dll` was already in `bin/` before this line,
          arriving through `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` → `Vixen.Editor.Core`. So it adds **zero** files
          to the output directory and only widens what this compilation may spell, which is what
          `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above otherwise hides.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Yaml\Vixen.Core.Yaml.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ Named rather than inherited, and that is what `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above
          costs: `Int2`, `Color4` and `CurveSample` used to arrive through `Vixen.Graphics`.
          `Vixen.Core` is where `Vixen.Core.Curves` lives, which is not obvious from the namespace.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the node-graph framework, which M1 deliberately left out and M4 cannot do without: a
          `TextureGraphCompiler` is a `NodeGraphCompiler<TexturePlan>`, and there is no way to derive
          from a type without referencing the assembly that declares it.

          ⚠ **What the omission bought is gone, and it was worth something.** This assembly's README
          says "does not reference `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` at all, which is what makes every test
          here a test of the evaluator" — after this line no test proves that by construction, and a
          plan-shaped assertion that quietly went through a node would look identical. What replaces
          it is a convention rather than a wall: the compiler suites build their own graphs and the
          evaluator suites still hand-build their plans, and the one differential test that crosses
          the line compares the two.

          ⚠ **And it makes this a UI assembly at runtime.** `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` references
          `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced`, `Vixen.Editor.Core` and `Vixen.Editor.Inspector` — its own
          csproj says so at length — so `Vixen.Ui.dll` is now in this assembly's output directory and
          anything that loads the evaluator loads the interface framework, including the content build
          that M5 will run headless. `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` narrows what this project
          *compiles* against and does nothing about that. Splitting the compiler into an assembly of
          its own is the fix — [#720](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/720).

          ⚠ **And the split is deliberately deferred, because no consumer would notice it yet.** All
          four projects that reference this one already load those eleven assemblies by another path
          — measured, and written down in the README's "No UI and no project" bullet along with what
          the move would cost. The consumer it is for is § M5's `vixen texture bake`; doing it before
          that caller exists is a seam built ahead of its caller rather than a fix for one.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the editor's project model, for `TextureProjectImages` —
          [#1087](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1087). An external image naming a project
          asset is resolved through an `AssetDatabase` and a `ProjectPaths`, and both are
          `Vixen.Editor.Core`'s.

          ⚠ **This costs nothing at runtime, which is the measurement the issue asked for rather
          than assumed.** `Vixen.Editor.Core` is already in this assembly's closure through
          `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` above — measured over every `ProjectReference` in the tree on
          2026-09-08, the closure is 32 assemblies with and without this line. What the line buys is
          the ability to *spell* `EditorProject`, which `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` was
          otherwise withholding.

          ⚠ **And it does not widen the compile surface to the interface framework.** With
          transitive references disabled, `Vixen.Editor.Core`'s own references — `Vixen.Ui` among
          them — stay unspellable here, so doc 48 § 4.1's refusal of an `Svg Path` node still rests
          on what this compilation may name.

          ⚠ **The reference this deliberately does not take is `Vixen.Editor.Assets`**, where
          `ImageDecoders` lives. Its closure is 54 and adds 25 assemblies to this one, including
          `Vixen.Engine`, `Vixen.Ecs`, `Vixen.Terrain`, `Vixen.Water`, `Vixen.Navigation` and
          `Vixen.Editor.VfxGraph`. That is why `TextureProjectImages` takes the decode as a delegate:
          the file-extension switch is the caller's, and everything that could disagree silently is
          not.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.Core\Vixen.Editor.Core.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **Doc 48 § 4.6's `Normal → Height` is a grid Poisson, and doc 42 § B1's solver is the
          solver.** `Vixen.Geometry.Uv/Solving` is a general sparse `SparseMatrix` /
          `ConjugateGradient` pair that happens to live behind an unwrapper's front door — warm
          started, with a fixed iteration budget rather than a residual test, which is exactly the
          determinism a bake wants. Its types are `internal`, so that assembly names this one in an
          `InternalsVisibleTo`; the wart and its fix are recorded there and in
          [#752](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/752).

          ⚠ **`Vixen.Core.Threading` is not optional even though nothing here starts a thread.**
          `ConjugateGradient.Solve` takes a `JobScheduler?` with a default of null, and C# cannot
          bind a call to a method whose parameter type the compilation cannot see — so leaving it out
          is a build error at the call site rather than a reference nobody uses. It costs two
          assemblies in the output directory, measured: `Vixen.Geometry` and `Vixen.Core.Threading`
          were the only names the closure did not already hold.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Geometry.Uv\Vixen.Geometry.Uv.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **The text stack, for doc 48 § 4.1's `Text`, and it costs nothing in the output
          directory.** Batch 5 refused `Core/Vixen.Ui` on a closure measurement — 20 projects against
          this assembly's 17 — and that measurement is now stale in both columns. Re-derived over
          every `ProjectReference` in the tree on 2026-09-05: this assembly's closure is **29**
          projects and **`Vixen.Ui` and `Vixen.Ui.Text` are already two of them**, arriving through
          the `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` reference above (→ `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced` → `Vixen.Ui`
          → `Vixen.Ui.Text`), whose own comment says as much. `Vixen.Ui`'s closure is 14 and a strict
          subset of those 29. So naming either adds **zero** files to `bin/`; what it changes is only
          what this compilation may spell, which `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above otherwise
          hides.

          Only the text half is named, because only the text half is used. Glyph outlines and a
          scanline fill are `Vixen.Ui.Text`; `SvgPath` and `PathFlattener` are `Vixen.Ui` proper, and
          § 4.1's `Svg Path` stays unwritten for a reason that is about a rasteriser rather than
          about a reference — [#753](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/753).
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Text\Vixen.Ui.Text.csproj" />

        <!--
          The node generator, over this assembly's own [Node] classes. Analyzers do not flow through
          a ProjectReference, so every assembly declaring nodes references it directly — which is
          also what a third-party plugin does.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
